GIT & GITHUB
Version Control Basics
============================================================

1. INTRODUCTION

1.1 What is Git?

Git is a version control system that tracks changes made to your code.

It allows you to:
- Track every change made to your project.
- Restore previous versions of your code.
- Experiment with new ideas without affecting existing code.
- Collaborate with other developers.

GitHub is an online platform used to store and manage Git repositories.

Think of Git as a save system for your code and GitHub as Google Drive for your code.

1.2 Why Do You Need Git?

As an AI Engineer, Git is an essential tool for managing projects.

Key benefits:

- Save Your Progress:
  Track and save your code changes so you don't lose your work.

- Experiment with Code:
  Try new ideas without breaking your existing working code.

- Share Projects:
  Share your projects with employers, developers, and collaborators.

- Access AI Projects:
  Many AI and Machine Learning projects, libraries, and open-source tools are available on GitHub.

1.3 Topics Covered

This section focuses on essential Git and GitHub concepts:

- Understanding Version Control (5 minutes)
- Creating a GitHub Account (5 minutes)
- Cloning and Creating Projects (10 minutes)
- Using Git in VS Code (10 minutes)


============================================================
2. VERSION CONTROL
============================================================

2.1 Understanding the Problem Git Solves

Imagine working on a Python project without using version control.

You might end up creating multiple files to maintain different versions of your code:

    my_script.py
    my_script_v2.py
    my_script_v2_final.py
    my_script_v2_final_ACTUALLY_FINAL.py
    my_script_backup_before_breaking_everything.py

This approach creates confusion and makes it difficult to identify the latest working version.

Git solves this problem by tracking changes systematically and maintaining the history of your project.

2.2 How Git Works

Think of Git as a system that takes snapshots of your code.

The basic process:

1. Write Code:
   Create or modify files in your project.

2. Save a Snapshot:
   Save the changes using a Git commit.

3. Track Changes:
   Git maintains the history of your commits, including information about who made the changes, when they were made, and the commit message.

Example:

Monday - Initial Code (20% Markup)

    def calculate_price(amount):
        return amount * 1.20  # 20% markup

Tuesday - Updated Code (25% Markup)

    def calculate_price(amount):
        return amount * 1.25  # Changed from 20% to 25%

Wednesday - Requirement Changed Back to 20%

The requirement is changed back to the original 20% markup.

With Git:
You can easily retrieve the previous version of the code.

Without Git:
You may need to search for an old backup file or manually rewrite the code.

This is one of the main advantages of version control.


============================================================
3. IMPORTANT GIT CONCEPTS
============================================================

The following are the essential Git concepts to understand.

3.1 Repository (Repo)

A repository is a project or directory that is tracked and managed by Git.

Example:

Your "python-for-ai" project folder can be initialized as a Git repository.

3.2 Staging

Staging means selecting which file changes should be included in the next commit.

Think of it as selecting items and placing them in a box before shipping.

Git command:

    git add .

3.3 Commit

A commit is a saved snapshot of the staged changes in your project.

It allows you to maintain a history of your code changes.

Think of it as saving progress in a video game. You can refer back to previous saved states.

Git command:

    git commit -m "Add price calculation function"

3.4 Push

Push uploads your local commits to a remote repository, such as GitHub.

It helps back up your code online and share changes with others.

Git command:

    git push

3.5 Pull

Pull downloads changes from a remote repository and integrates them into your local repository.

It is useful when working with other developers.

Git command:

    git pull

3.6 Clone

Clone creates a local copy of an existing remote Git repository.

It is commonly used to download projects from GitHub.

Git command:

    git clone <repository-url>


