============================================================
5. BASIC GIT WORKFLOW
============================================================

5.1 The Simplest Git Workflow

The following commands are sufficient to understand the basic Git workflow.

IMPORTANT:
These are terminal commands, not Python code.

Execute them in the VS Code terminal.

Step 0: Initialize Git (First Time Only)

Navigate to your project directory and execute:

    git init

This initializes a new Git repository in the current project directory.

Note:
This command only needs to be executed once when setting up Git for a project.

Step 1: Stage Your Changes

    git add .

This stages all changes in the current directory for the next commit.

Step 2: Commit Your Changes

    git commit -m "Add price calculation function"

This creates a commit containing the staged changes.

The commit message describes what was changed.

Step 3: Push Your Changes to GitHub

    git push

This uploads your local commits to GitHub.

Note:
Before pushing, the local repository must be connected to a remote GitHub repository, and the necessary upstream configuration must be set.

5.2 Complete Basic Workflow

The basic sequence is:

    git init
    git add .
    git commit -m "Add price calculation function"
    git push

Explanation:

- git init: Initialize the repository (first time only).
- git add .: Stage the changes.
- git commit: Save the changes locally.
- git push: Upload the commits to GitHub.

IMPORTANT:
If you encounter the following error:

    fatal: not a git repository

It means the current directory is not recognized as a Git repository.

Solution:

1. Navigate to your project directory.
2. Execute:

       git init

3. Continue with the Git workflow.

Note:
You only need to initialize the repository once per project.

5.3 Executing Git Commands

You can execute Git commands in two ways:

Option 1: Execute Commands One by One

- Type one command in the terminal.
- Press Enter.
- Wait for the command to finish.
- Execute the next command.

Option 2: Execute Commands Together

- Copy multiple commands.
- Paste them into the terminal.
- Press Enter.

The terminal executes the commands sequentially.


