GITHUB SETUP
Creating Your GitHub Account
============================================================

1. INTRODUCTION

1.1 What is GitHub Setup?

GitHub is an online platform used to store, manage, and share Git repositories.

Creating a GitHub account is an essential step in managing your code and collaborating with other developers.

GitHub is free to get started and takes approximately 5 minutes to set up.

1.2 Why Do You Need a GitHub Account?

As an AI Engineer, you will use GitHub for the following purposes:

- Back Up Your Code:
  Store your projects online so you don't lose your work.

- Share Your Projects:
  Share your code with employers, developers, and collaborators.

- Access AI Tools:
  Download open-source AI tools, libraries, and example projects.

============================================================
2. CREATE YOUR GITHUB ACCOUNT
============================================================

Step 1: Sign Up

1. Visit the official GitHub website:
   https://github.com/

2. Click the "Sign up" button.

3. Enter the following details:

   Username:
   Choose a professional username because employers and collaborators may see it.

   Email:
   Use an email address that you check regularly.

   Password:
   Create a strong and secure password.

Step 2: Verify Your Account

1. GitHub will send a verification email to your registered email address.

2. Open your email inbox.

3. Find the verification email from GitHub.

4. Click the verification link to verify your account.

Step 3: Set Up Your GitHub Profile

After logging in to GitHub:

1. Click your profile picture in the top-right corner.

2. Select "Your profile".

3. Click "Edit profile".

4. Add the following information:

   Name:
   Enter your real name.

   Profile Picture:
   Upload a professional profile picture.

   Bio:
   Add a short description about yourself.

   Example:
   "Learning Python for AI Development"

Note:
Keep your GitHub profile professional because it can be viewed by employers and other developers.

============================================================
3. CONFIGURE GIT LOCALLY
============================================================

3.1 Why Configure Git?

Git needs to know the name and email address of the person creating commits.

This information is associated with your Git commits.

You only need to configure these details once on your local machine.

3.2 Configure Git Username and Email

Step 1: Open VS Code.

Step 2: Open the terminal.

    Terminal > New Terminal

Step 3: Execute the following commands.

Configure your Git username:

    git config --global user.name "Your Name"

Configure your Git email:

    git config --global user.email "your.email@example.com"

IMPORTANT:
Use the same email address associated with your GitHub account.

3.3 Verify Git Configuration

To verify the configured username:

    git config --global user.name

To verify the configured email:

    git config --global user.email

These commands display the username and email configured in Git.

============================================================
4. GITHUB AUTHENTICATION METHODS
============================================================

4.1 What is Authentication?

Authentication is the process of verifying your identity when connecting your local Git repository to GitHub.

GitHub supports different authentication methods.

The three commonly used approaches are:

1. GitHub CLI (gh)
2. HTTPS with Personal Access Token
3. SSH Keys

RECOMMENDATION:

GitHub CLI is recommended for beginners because it provides a simple authentication experience and can manage GitHub credentials automatically.

============================================================
5. GITHUB CLI (RECOMMENDED)
============================================================

5.1 What is GitHub CLI?

GitHub CLI (gh) is the official command-line tool provided by GitHub.

It allows you to interact with GitHub directly from your terminal.

It simplifies authentication and other GitHub-related operations.

5.2 Install GitHub CLI

GitHub CLI can be installed on different operating systems.

Windows:

1. Visit the official website:
   https://cli.github.com/

2. Download the Windows installer.

3. Run the installer and complete the installation.

macOS:

Execute the following command:

    brew install gh

Linux:

Follow the official installation guide:

    https://github.com/cli/cli#installation

5.3 Authenticate Using GitHub CLI

After installing GitHub CLI:

Step 1: Open the VS Code terminal.

Step 2: Execute the following command:

    gh auth login

Step 3: Follow the instructions displayed in the terminal.

Select the following options:

1. Choose GitHub.com.
2. Choose an authentication protocol:
   - SSH (Recommended)
   - HTTPS
3. Select browser-based authentication when prompted.
4. Complete the authentication process in your browser.
5. Authorize GitHub CLI if requested.

Once authentication is successful, GitHub CLI stores and manages the necessary authentication credentials.

Git operations can then use the configured GitHub authentication.

============================================================
6. AUTHENTICATION SUMMARY
============================================================

Available GitHub authentication methods:

1. GitHub CLI (gh)
   - Official GitHub command-line tool.
   - Simplifies the authentication process.
   - Recommended for beginners.

2. HTTPS with Personal Access Token
   - Uses HTTPS to connect to GitHub.
   - Requires a Personal Access Token for authentication.

3. SSH Keys
   - Uses a public and private key pair.
   - Allows secure authentication without entering credentials for every connection.

Recommended Approach:

Use GitHub CLI (gh) for a simple initial setup experience.

